{"v":1,"accession":"PRJNA1258700","title":"Waste activated sludge treatment with electrochemical pretreatment and anaerobic fermentation","description":"Waste activated sludge treatment is essential in wastewater management, focusing on reducing sludge volume and enhancing biodegradability. A novel approach combines electrochemical pretreatment with anaerobic fermentation, optimizing the treatment process and enabling the recovery of volatile fatty acids.The process is further enhanced by adding electrolytes to the sludge, which improves conductivity and increases VFA recovery efficiency. Increased conductivity not only boosts electrochemical reaction effectiveness but also maximizes the solubilization of organic materials, benefiting subsequent anaerobic fermentation.This study explores the innovative use of brine waste as an electrolyte source in the treatment of waste activated sludge, addressing the challenge of effective sludge management and resource recovery. By employing a combination of electrochemical pretreatment and anaerobic fermentation, the study utilizes brine waste equivalent to different concentrations of chloride to enhance the conductivity of the sludge as well as VFA production. By demonstrating this viable method of waste treatment and resource recovery, the study seeks to provide a sustainable and adaptable solution for wastewater treatment facilities worldwide.","center":"The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","submission_date":"2025/05/04 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMN48328310","Metagenome or environmental sample from activated sludge metagenome","uncertain",null,null,2024,"HK",null,1819729,"Hong Kong","probable",22.3193,114.1694,"Hong Kong